<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mission San Luis de Talimali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1656–1704</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mission San Luis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mission San Luis de Talimali was a Spanish Franciscan mission in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallahassee">Tallahassee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, founded in 1656 among the Apalachee. It became the western capital of Spanish Florida, where Apalachee leaders lived alongside Spanish soldiers, friars and settlers. It was abandoned and burned in 1704 as raids from Carolina destroyed the mission system, and the reconstructed site is now a museum.</p></div>
